# Line endings, so that a clone on any machine produces the same bytes.
#
# This machine (and most Windows ones) has core.autocrlf=true, which
# rewrites checked-out text files to CRLF. That silently breaks two
# different parts of this homework, in ways gcc will not warn about:
#
#   1. *.sh and Makefile
#      sh reads regress.sh line by line, so a trailing \r makes dash
#      report "Syntax error: word unexpected (expecting do)" on the
#      first `for` loop, and `make test` fails before running anything.
#
#   2. data/*.txt and expected/*.txt
#      regress.sh does `diff - expected/<name>`. The program writes
#      LF on stdout, so a CRLF copy of the golden file never matches.
#      Worse, the *input* files matter too: a blank line read as
#      "\r\n" is no longer recognised as blank, it fails to parse as
#      three numbers, and the printed `skipped:` count changes.
#      So the outputs would differ even with the diff removed.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf on checkout: text is always LF on disk,
# on every platform. This applies to every text file so that a future
# homework's data files are covered without editing this file.
#
# `.zip` is explicitly not text so that the starter archive is stored
# and checked out byte for byte. Verified by cloning this repository
# with autocrlf=true: Makefile, *.sh, data/*.txt and expected/*.txt
# contain no CR bytes, the .zip is bit-identical, and `make test`
# reports 4/4 PASS.
*           text=auto eol=lf
*.zip       -text
